{"requirements":[{"id":"rsa-noninteractive-local-ssh","requirement":"On node1, opsadmin can authenticate non-interactively to the local host using an RSA key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A 3072-bit RSA key was created for opsadmin, its public key was installed as authorized_keys with restrictive ownership, modes, and SELinux labels, and direct non-interactive SSH completed as opsadmin. The same automatic workflow succeeded after restart and in the final audit.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9e93d91d86b749e4","cmd-1535a83a810d423c","cmd-2a10c1218b944e51","cmd-d0f8ac7d49824434","cmd-2c6ca34f0081472d"]},{"id":"strict-host-key-pinning","requirement":"Pin the server host key for opsadmin so an unexpected host key is rejected without prompting.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin's client configuration pins the server's ED25519, ECDSA, and RSA keys and enables BatchMode, StrictHostKeyChecking, and disabled host-key updates. Deliberately mismatched keys were rejected with exit 255 and host-key verification errors both before and after restart, without timing out or prompting.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6d8e46948a854cb8","cmd-fb0125a309324e98","cmd-e74924a69a534fff","cmd-2c6ca34f0081472d"]},{"id":"preserve-platform","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The system remained RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0 and the existing RHEL OpenSSH 9.9p1 packages. System SSH configuration checksums remained unchanged, package health was intact, and no replacement software or platform upgrade was introduced.","evidence_ids":["cmd-55fadaacbd4e4e8c","cmd-d0f8ac7d49824434","cmd-2c6ca34f0081472d","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Account creation, RSA credentials, authorized_keys, known_hosts, and client policy directly implemented the requested outcome. Both temporary mismatched-key test directories were bounded evidence probes and were removed; the final audit and lifecycle snapshot show no test residue. The reboot mutation was required by the provider-wide restart protocol and was followed by healthy recovery. No failed services, new listeners, package damage, weakened controls, or unrelated retained changes were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-37dfd4e56a0a4a40","cmd-9e93d91d86b749e4","cmd-6d8e46948a854cb8","cmd-fb0125a309324e98","cmd-f4c7e51e70184ab5","cmd-e74924a69a534fff","cmd-2c6ca34f0081472d","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory node-restart protocol is also complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, the restart was acknowledged and observed through loss of connectivity, the post-restart boot ID differed, and system, sshd, credential, pinning, and login state recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Lifecycle snapshots are bounded supplemental observations rather than a complete filesystem inventory, but the task-specific final commands directly verify the relevant account, SSH files, behavior, platform, and cleanup state."]}
